<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ripuarian Franks</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ripuarians, Rhineland Franks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ripuarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were the Franks of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, who took over the city as Roman rule collapsed in the mid-5th century. They had their own kings, the last being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigibert-the-lame">Sigibert the Lame</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chloderic">Chloderic</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> absorbed their kingdom around 509. Within the Frankish realm they kept their own law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-ripuaria">Lex Ripuaria</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ripuarian Franks, also called Rhenish or Rhineland Franks, were the Franks who established themselves in and around the formerly Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Until the 1950s they were seen as the easternmost of two distinct sub-tribes of the Franks, ruling much of what later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, while their western counterparts, the Salians, took northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. This view rested on two 7th-century codes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-ripuaria">Lex Ripuaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Lex Salica</a>. Since the 1950s the term Ripuarian is no longer seen as having begun as a tribal name, even if it came to name a specific population based at Cologne.</p><p>The origin of the word is debated. A traditional explanation derives it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ripa, riverbank, referring to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, but the second part of the name resembles the Germanic suffix -varii, meaning inhabitants, found in tribal names such as the Ampsivarii. Springer proposed that the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> owed the name to a unit of Roman border police posted there before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> lost its independence almost as soon as it entered the record. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, Cologne was ruled by Sigobert the Lame, who had fought beside the Salian Frank Clovis against the Alamanni and was lamed by a wound at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolbiac">Battle of Tolbiac</a> in 496. In 509, Gregory says, Clovis sent word to Sigobert's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chloderic">Chloderic</a> that he would ally with him if his father died. Chloderic had his father killed by hired assassins in his tent across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> from Cologne, and then offered Clovis part of the treasury. Chloderic was killed by an axe blow as he showed it to Clovis's messengers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> then came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, denied any part in the murders and advised the citizens to place themselves under his protection. They acclaimed him by voice vote and raised him on their shields, and the independent kingdom of the Cologne Franks ended at a single assembly in 509.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the first half of the 7th century the Ripuarians received the Ripuarian law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-ripuaria">Lex Ripuaria</a>, from the dominant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a>, who mainly re-authorized laws the Ripuarians already used so that they kept a local constitution. It is now seen mainly as a law code of the Cologne Rhineland, covering neighbouring towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a>, Jülich and Neuss. The earliest narrative source clearly using the word for the people of Cologne is the Liber Historiae Francorum, completed about 726/27. The term was later used of larger areas: the Annals of Xanten called Lothar II king of the Ripuarians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ripuarian Franks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Roman writers speak of several kings, each with his own people and war band, and by the 5th century two main groupings can be distinguished: the Salian Franks of the north-west, in Toxandria and later around Tournai and Cambrai, and the <b>Rhineland Franks</b>, later called Ripuarians, around Cologne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franks of the middle Rhine, later called <b>Ripuarian Franks</b>, took over Cologne in the mid 5th century as Roman power collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cologne">Kingdom of Cologne</a></li></ul></section>
